Multi-Converter can use bundled third-party conversion engines. Those engines
are separate third-party software packages and retain their own licenses,
notices, and redistribution conditions.

Current desktop releases bundle FFmpeg and ffprobe as platform-specific base
sidecars, and bundle advanced engines such as PDFium, LibreOffice, Pandoc, and
libvips as application resources when validated for that platform. The Windows
x64 package uses FFmpeg/ffprobe 8.1.1-essentials_build-www.gyan.dev, configured
with --enable-gpl, so those Windows executables are treated as GPL-covered
third-party software. Multi-Converter uses these engines as separate local
processes or libraries; the AGPL-3.0-or-later license for Multi-Converter does
not replace their licenses.

Each engine archive must include the applicable license files and, when
required or configured, third-party notices for the engine and its bundled
components. The AGPL-3.0-or-later license for Multi-Converter does not automatically
apply to FFmpeg, ffprobe, PDFium, LibreOffice, Pandoc, libvips, 7-Zip,
or any other third-party engine bundled or distributed for Multi-Converter.

See docs/THIRD_PARTY_ENGINES.md for the V1 engine notice checklist and
redistribution warnings.

The PDFium conversion/OCR wrapper uses pdfium-render 0.9.1 under
MIT OR Apache-2.0 and lopdf 0.42.0 under MIT. It provides bounded PDF page
inspection, text extraction and rendering for conversion and OCR. Each crate
retains its own license. Document/PDF editors and their frontend dependencies
are no longer included in V1.0.7.

The native conversion application uses base64 0.22.1 under MIT OR Apache-2.0
and roxmltree 0.21.1 under MIT OR Apache-2.0. Exact dependency versions and
checksums are pinned in the Cargo locks; upstream license texts remain in the
corresponding dependency source distributions.

The unreleased V1.0.7 development build includes a local official
PP-OCRv6_medium runtime composed from PaddlePaddle 3.3.1, PaddleOCR 3.7.0 and
PaddleX 3.7.0. Its Python distributions retain their own licenses. Exact
versions, artifact hashes and license-file hashes are recorded in
`tools/ocr-runtime/requirements-windows-x64.lock.txt` and
`src-tauri/ocr-runtime-licenses.json`. Native Linux and Apple Silicon inventory
receipts are in `docs/audits/2026-10-30/*-ocr-runtime-licenses.json`; each packaged
runtime includes its matching inventory and license files. Platform inventories
differ and must not be substituted for one another.

`bce-python-sdk` 0.9.76 declares Apache License 2.0 but its exact wheel and
source archive contain no license file. Multi-Converter packages the canonical
Apache-2.0 text separately at
`src-tauri/ocr-resources/licenses/bce-python-sdk-0.9.76.LICENSE.txt`; exact
artifact, license-origin and SHA-256 values are recorded in
`tools/ocr-runtime/supplemental-licenses.json`. This engineering record does
not replace maintainer legal review before public redistribution.

